+# MiniCPM-V 4.7 35B-A3B LES OpenAI-compatible bridge validation
+
+Date: 2026-10-14, Apple M4 Mac mini 16 GB, macOS, local loopback only.
+
+## Runtime setup
+
+- Upstream runtime: `tc-mb/llama.cpp-omni`, branch `ios-omni`, original source HEAD `96bbf61`.
+- Engine: independently compiled Metal `llama-cli`, split GGUF full 256 routed experts, top-8, bank 128, ubatch 16, context 4096, LES prefetch off, decoder window off.
+- API: Python standard-library local bridge, listening only on `127.0.0.1:18792`.
+- Supported endpoints: GET `/health`, GET `/v1/models`, POST `/v1/chat/completions` (`stream:false`), one function call/response per HTTP request.
+- Tools executed by **separate allowlisted host test harness**, never by bridge.
+
+## Validation
+
+- HTTP /health and /v1/models: PASS.
+- Single completion with `tool_choice=required`: HTTP 200, native OpenAI-style `message.tool_calls` with `run_tests` arguments `{}`, ~27 seconds.
+- Multi-turn agent test from `agent-bench/real_tool_loop.py`: **PASS** in 555.3 seconds, six assistant turns, five true tool calls.
+- Tool sequence: `run_tests` (initial failure) -> `read_file(source.py)` -> `replace_text` (model emitted flat top-level args; old adapter forwarded `{}` and host safely rejected) -> `replace_text` (model recovered with nested arguments; host applied) -> `run_tests` (6/6 PASS), followed by assistant final success summary.
+- Independently verified final sandbox tests: **6/6 PASS**.
+- Local bridge unit tests: **8/8 PASS** including both nested/flat tool arguments, parser behavior and enum rejection.
+- Observed macOS swap: ~301.69 MB already allocated before benchmark; **no increase observed**, not a claim of 0 system swap.
+
+## Implementation notes
+
+1. `server.py` provides limited OpenAI-style response shape, with strict host binding and serial request handling; it does not invoke tools itself.
+2. Parser recognizes both `{"tool":"x","arguments":{...}}` and `{"tool":"x","path":"...",...}` emitted by the model. Corrected compatibility code was unit tested **after** the successful agent run; the original run demonstrated model recovery from the old adapter's rejection.
+3. The `ios-omni` branch has `tools/server/server.cpp`, but its legacy `utils.hpp` references removed types (`common_remote_params`, `thinking_forced_open`); a native server was **not** completed. The exploratory CMake change was reverted.
+4. Every turn launches a new `llama-cli` process, re-encodes full message transcript, no persistent KV cache, no true streaming, no token accounting. The six-turn wall time is therefore a bridge upper-bound and **not** a benchmark of a native resident server.
+5. The text inference is functional for coding; this integration test did not validate image handling or all OpenAI request variants.
+
+## Evidence and next steps
+
+- Detailed tool logs: `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/real_agent_session.json`
+- Patched sandbox: `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/real-agent-sandboxes/session/`
+- API response: `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/api_smoke.json`
+- API logs: `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/bridge.log`
+- Test fixture: `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/tasks/session/`
+
+Next: native resident OpenAI server using current `server-context`, so weights and KV context survive tool calls; then agent task suite over multiple seeds, long context and parameter A/B. Do **not** compare ~555s process-per-turn directly with Qwen persistent llama-server results as equal-engine inference performance.
